<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William Hobson</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1792">1792</a>–<a href="/years/1842">1842</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William Hobson was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> officer and the first governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>. Sent by the British government, he negotiated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-waitangi">Treaty of Waitangi</a> with Māori chiefs on 6 February 1840 and proclaimed British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> over the islands. He chose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auckland">Auckland</a> as capital and governed the new colony until his death in office in 1842.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-hobson.jpg" alt="William Hobson" width="320" height="422" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: McDonald, James Ingram, 1865-1935; Collins, James Edgell, 1820 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:William_Hobson_portrait_by_James_Ingram_McDonald_1913.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>William Hobson was born on 26 September 1792 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waterford">Waterford</a>, in the Kingdom of Ireland, the son of Samuel Hobson, a barrister, and Martha Jones, and grew up in an Anglo-Irish Anglican family. He entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> as a Volunteer 2nd Class on 25 August 1803, served in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a> and the War of 1812, became a midshipman in 1806 and was commissioned first lieutenant on 11 November 1813.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Much of his early service was against piracy in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>. On 29 September 1822 his sloop Whim was captured off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> by the pirate leader Pepe Diablito; he and his men were mistreated, then set adrift without provisions and rescued days later by Lieutenant Hugh Nurse of the Eliza. In 1823, commanding the schooner HMS Lion, he took a series of pirate vessels, which brought him promotion to commander on 18 March 1824 and the nickname Lion Hobson.</p><p>In command of HMS Ferret he captured the Spanish schooner Fingal on 3 February 1826 with 58 enslaved people from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> aboard; the Mixed Commission Court condemned the vessel and ordered their emancipation. He married Eliza Ann Elliott at Nassau in December 1827 and was made post captain from 9 July 1828. In 1837 he sailed in HMS Rattlesnake to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> after news of an inter-tribal Maori war, and his report proposed British enclaves there on the model of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hudsons-bay-company">Hudson's Bay Company</a>.</p><p>In 1839 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> appointed him consul and lieutenant-governor under George Gipps, governor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sydney-nova-scotia">Sydney</a>, with instructions to treat with the Maori for the cession of the islands. He reached the Bay of Islands aboard HMS Herald on 29 January 1840, helped draft the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-waitangi">Treaty of Waitangi</a> and headed its British signatories, saying to each chief who signed, He iwi tahi tātou, we are now one people. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crown-colony">Crown colony</a> he was sworn in as governor on 3 May 1841.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Hobson suffered a second stroke and died on 10 September 1842. He was buried in the Symonds Street cemetery in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auckland">Auckland</a> on 13 September with military honours. Many Māori attended, performing uhunga, expressions of grief, as for one of their own great chiefs. Hobsonville, Hobson Bay, Mount Hobson and streets in Auckland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wellington">Wellington</a> are named after him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “William Hobson” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The British government sent Captain <b>William Hobson</b> to negotiate with Māori, and on 6 February 1840 the Treaty of Waitangi was signed at Waitangi; during the year more than 500 rangatira signed it.</blockquote><a href="/culture/maori">Māori · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>It became a separate colony in 1841 under Governor <b>William Hobson</b>, with Auckland as capital until Wellington replaced it in 1865.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-new-zealand">Colony of New Zealand</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waterford">Waterford</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/ireland">Ireland</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auckland">Auckland</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.26,-7.12&amp;year=1824" aria-label="Waterford on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4390 359 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of William Hobson"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4840 629h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4840 629h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.26,-7.12&amp;year=1824">What was here in 1824? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
